Subject: Weird netgroup problem in /etc/exports between OSF and Linux
Date: 1997/11/05
Message-ID: <63qsuh$4kb$3@nntp.Stanford.EDU>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 287259146
Followup-To: comp.os.linux.networking
Organization: Department of Structural Biology, Stanford University
Reply-To: expt@alanine.ram.org
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.networking,comp.os.linux.misc,comp.unix.osf.misc


Our system is a bunch of DECs running OSF (NFS version 2), SGIs
running Irix, and Intel and Alpha boxes running Linux.  The exports
file in the Linux boxes is of this form:

/  @group(rw)

where "group" is a NIS domain, defined in /etc/netgroups on the
ypmaster, and locally, and visible by typing ypcat /etc/netgroups. 

Once all the exports are in this form, and all the hosts belong to the
yp group, all the SGI and Linux boxes can mount each other through the
automounter by typing:

% cd /hosts/<host>

where <host> is the Linux box.  However, an OSF box cannot use the
automounter to do this UNLESS its hostname is explicitly specified in
the /etc/exports file on the server side:

/ hostname(rw)

HOWEVER, the OSF box can still mount the remote NFS by:

% mount -t nfs linux_host:/ /mnt

and it will work perfectly. 

So there's something that the automount is doing that's different from
what the NFS client on the OSF boxes is doing. Can anyone shed any
light on this problem? The automount options on the OSF boxes is:

-v /hosts -hosts -intr,nosuid,nodev,timeo=1,vers=2

(The vers=2 is required so it can work with the SGIs.)

